{"name":"Black Walnut","hex":"#5e4f46","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black walnut","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Juglans nigra, a tree native to eastern North America.","The nuts of the aforementioned tree."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black_walnut"}],"entries":[{"title":"Black Walnut (Clover, Virginia)","blocks":[{"text":"Black Walnut is a historic plantation house and farm located near Clover, Halifax County, Virginia. The main house was built in at least three sections beginning about 1774 to 1790. In the 1840s and 1850s, a substantial two-story frame addition was built in two stages parallel to the existing house, along with a connecting hyphen, altogether giving the house an H-shape. The interior features Greek Revival style details."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Black_Walnut_Manor_House.jpg/330px-Black_Walnut_Manor_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Walnut_(Clover%2C_Virginia)","wikidata":"Q14712032"},{"title":"Juglans nigra","blocks":[{"text":"Juglans nigra, the eastern American black walnut, is a species of deciduous tree in the walnut family, Juglandaceae, native to central and eastern North America, growing mostly in riparian zones."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Black_Walnut_nut_and_leave_detail.JPG/330px-Black_Walnut_nut_and_leave_detail.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juglans_nigra","wikidata":"Q852572"},{"title":"Walnut","blocks":[{"text":"A walnut is the edible seed of any tree of the genus Juglans, most often the Persian or English walnut, Juglans regia. They are accessory fruit because the outer covering of the fruit is technically an involucre and thus not morphologically part of the carpel; this means it cannot be a drupe but is instead a drupe-like nut."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Walnuts_-_whole_and_open_with_halved_kernel.jpg/330px-Walnuts_-_whole_and_open_with_halved_kernel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walnut","wikidata":"Q208021"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black","wikidata":"Q23445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Brick_Kitchen.jpeg/500px-Brick_Kitchen.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Brick_Kitchen.jpeg/500px-Brick_Kitchen.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Brick Kitchen, Built ~1800","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABrick_Kitchen.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Black_Walnut_Plantation_School_House.jpg/500px-Black_Walnut_Plantation_School_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Black_Walnut_Plantation_School_House.jpg/500px-Black_Walnut_Plant